RustDesk 1.4.9 for the Neutrino Android app: a C interface to one remote
desktop session, for a host with no Dart. Applied by build_core_rustdesk.py with
`patch -p1` at the root of the source tree.

Cargo.toml: the `native` feature, which turns `flutter` on. The client,
server and keyboard code call into src/flutter.rs and src/flutter_ffi.rs in
some thirty places, so those two modules stay compiled; they need the
flutter_rust_bridge crate and no Dart.

src/lib.rs: under `native`, `bridge_generated` is not compiled (it is the
Dart bridge flutter_rust_bridge_codegen generates, not in the source tree),
and `native_ffi` is.

src/client/io_loop.rs: under `native`, the remote clipboard's text goes to
the session's `clipboard` callback, as on iOS, and not to the Java clipboard
manager the Flutter app registers.

src/native_ffi.rs: the interface. `NativeHandler` implements the session
callbacks (`InvokeUiSession`); every frame is drawn into the window given by
`nd_attach_window`, and the login, the end of the connection, each frame
size and the remote clipboard's text go to the host's callbacks. `nd_start`
turns the peer's clipboard option on and applies the options `nd_set_option`
kept (the image quality, and peer options such as the codec preference), and
`nd_clipboard` sends the host's clipboard text to the remote machine.
`nd_init`, `nd_set_option`, `nd_start`, `nd_attach_window`, `nd_mouse`,
`nd_key`, `nd_text`, `nd_clipboard` and `nd_close` are exported with C
linkage.
In a build with the `mediacodec` feature, `nd_init` starts RustDesk's check of
the device's MediaCodec H264 and H265 decoders and logs what it found, and a
session waits up to 2 s for that check before it connects.

libs/scrap/src/common/mediacodec.rs: the MediaCodec decoder, which RustDesk's
own Android build does not compile, made to compile and to draw. The check
creates one H264 and one H265 decoder instead of calling a function the tree
no longer has, logs the result, and marks itself done. A decoded frame reads
its crop, stride, slice height and colour format from the output format,
converts planar (I420) and semi-planar (NV12) output with the matching libyuv
call into ARGB or ABGR, sets the frame's width and height, and logs the colour
format once per change; an unknown colour format fails that frame.
It also implements the two Dart conversion traits for `EventToUI` that the
generated bridge otherwise implements; no Dart stream is opened in this
build.

